  "query_preview": "../Wednesday through Monday/ Upper level features show \na rather active pattern through the long range period. We start \nout with overall zonal W to E flow over much of the region that \nwill gradually transition to a broad upper trough stretching from\nthe Russian Far East to NW Canada by the weekend. The transition\nis set in motion by an upper low moving out of Russia late week \nthat eventually anchors itself over SW mainland Alaska. The broad\ntrough stays in place into early next week, but several short \nwaves are evident rotating around the southern edge of it.\n\nAt the surface, this translates into a rather active pattern for\nSE AK. First, we are still expecting a front to arrive Wednesday\nfor the northern half of the panhandle bringing a batch of rain\nand wind with it. The wind forecast has not really changed with\nthe highest winds still expected in the northern inner channels\naround Wednesday morning (20 to 30 kt southerly winds, highest in\nnorthern Lynn). The rain forecast however does have a few changes.\nThe surface ridge that is still over the eastern gulf at this time\nis expected to cause the front to rapidly weaken as it moves east.\nSo while Yakutat is still likely to see moderate to heavy rain\nrates for a time (rain totals around 1.5 inches), areas farther\neast are expected to receive not as much (around an inch at most\nfor the northern panhandle through late Wed night). After this\nfront, Thursday may offer some break in the rainfall, but with\ncontinued onshore flow clouds and showers will still be around for\nmost areas. Into late week and the weekend, the forecast has\nbecome more active, now featuring a front coming into the\npanhandle on Friday and Friday night with a following front coming\nin late Saturday. Needless to say that the general trend is toward\na more active and wetter weather pattern into next weekend. With\nall the clouds and rainfall around, temperatures will also show a\ntrend toward cooling more toward normal temperatures for this time\nof year across the entire panhandle. In this case it means high\ntemperatures in the mid 50s to near 60 for most areas.",
